{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 110,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has been said that scientific progress\nis accompanied not by cries of “Eureka”,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4900,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead by murmurs of “huh, that’s\nweird”. Well, we’ve just observed a faint"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10280,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point of light on the sky whose weirdness\ncould change the way we think about the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15119,
    "dDurationMs": 1786,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the largest scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21730,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This particular “huh, that’s weird”\ntakes the form of a white dwarf star that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26340,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing some stuff that no white dwarf should ever be able to do. In fact, it has multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31120,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties that are so extreme that it almost certainly did NOT form in the way that we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35820,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thought all white dwarfs formed. This one peculiar point of faint light may change our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41650,
    "dDurationMs": 5819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of not just white dwarfs, but\nof all cosmology. Mysterious, huh?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47469,
    "dDurationMs": 6272,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This particular mystery began at the Zwicky Transient Facility in California - a telescope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53741,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dedicated to watching for things that go bump in the night - astrophysical objects that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59260,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vary over time. Astronomers using the ZTF\ncaught a white dwarf that, at first glance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65460,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looked suspicious - a bit too massive and\nspinning a bit too fast. That spin was seen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70560,
    "dDurationMs": 2497,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in its rapid but periodic flickering - suggesting that it was rotating several times every minute."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73057,
    "dDurationMs": 8083,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It got the poetic name ZTF J1901+1458 - we’ll call it Zee for short."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81140,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a criminal investigation, a suspect must\nbe treated as innocent until proven guilty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86409,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a scientific investigation we should assume a thing to be typical until proven weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91890,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, follow-up observations proved Zee weird beyond reasonable doubt. Observations with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97400,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Hale Telescope confirmed that it’s 1)\ndefinitely a white dwarf, and 2) definitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102810,
    "dDurationMs": 2349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spinning way too fast to make sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105159,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get into why this is so weird, let’s review what we know about white dwarfs - or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109840,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least, what we thought we knew. When all but the most massive stars end their lives,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114439,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they blast off their outer layers in their\nfinal fits of nuclear fusion. This exposes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119570,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their naked cores, which by now are insanely hot nuggets of nuclear ash - hyper-dense balls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125130,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of mostly carbon and oxygen. These are white dwarfs, the final fate of any star less than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131180,
    "dDurationMs": 2529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "8 or so times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133709,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s how we thought all white dwarfs were formed. But something is off with Zee - particularly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139450,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with how fast it’s spinning. Now we do expect white dwarfs to rotate. After all, stars rotate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144930,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so should their remnant cores. That spin should also increase as the core slowly collapses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150370,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under its own gravitational crush due to conservation of angular momentum. But typical white dwarfs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155650,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take from a few hours to a few days to rotate. But it’s hard to see how any star could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161090,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be rotating fast enough to produce a white dwarf that spins every 7 minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167530,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a parent star should have torn itself\nto pieces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171420,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so the mystery deepens. At this point, astronomers decided that the object was weird enough to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176510,
    "dDurationMs": 6601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bring some serious firepower to the investigation - the W. M. Keck Telescope in Hawaii, one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183111,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the largest telescopes in the world. Keck was needed to do the spectroscopy - to break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188660,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the white dwarf’s light up into component colours. Splitting the light this way makes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193209,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it even harder to detect this already faint\nobject, hence the need for a giant telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199250,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s worth it because spectroscopy can\nyield an enormous amount of information - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203910,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a full forensic workup. For example, it gives us spectral lines. When electrons in an atom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210440,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "move between orbitals, they emit or absorb light with very specific wavelengths. That"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215890,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us what kind of atoms are in the object, but also a lot more. In the case of Zee the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221640,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelengths of the hydrogen absorption lines were shuffled all over the place in a way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226580,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that suggests the presence of gigantic magnetic fields. Fields around a billion times stronger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232040,
    "dDurationMs": 5919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the earth or sun’s magnetic field.\nThat’s at the top tier of the most magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237959,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "white dwarfs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238959,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so far so weird. The next step in the\nstar-sleuth’s playbook is to get an accurate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243980,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure of size. Size is hard to measure even for normal stars: most are so far away that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250239,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even our highest-resolution telescope cameras see them as single points of light. But astronomers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255530,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a clever trick. If you know how much\nlight a star is churning out - its luminosity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260780,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- then you can figure out how big it needs\nto be in order to shine that brightly. You"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265461,
    "dDurationMs": 4798,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also need to know how efficiently it’s shining - how much energy for every unit of surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270259,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area - but that’s just a function of its\ntemperature, and you can measure temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275159,
    "dDurationMs": 1891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the star’s color."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277050,
    "dDurationMs": 5619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Temperature is surprisingly easy to measure, but luminosity is less so. Luminosity determines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282669,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how bright the star appears to us - but there’s another factor at play there - how far away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287839,
    "dDurationMs": 5491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star is. Measure the star’s brightness\non the sky, factor in how much that brightness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293330,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been dimmed by distance and you have its luminosity. Then luminosity plus temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300210,
    "dDurationMs": 1579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives the star’s size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301789,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the only thing we’re missing from this\nequation for size is the distance to this star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307009,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and of all of these things distance is the hardest to measure. The most accurate way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311669,
    "dDurationMs": 4762,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get distances to stars is with stellar\nparallax. That’s when the motion of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316431,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth causes a star to appear to move relative to more distant stars. Until recently it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321520,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only been possible to do this for the most nearby stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324110,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the European Space Agency’s GAIA satellite changed that by measuring parallaxes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329909,
    "dDurationMs": 6361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a billion stars across the Milky Way,\nand Zee was one of them. So we have its distance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336270,
    "dDurationMs": 3359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- around 135 lightyears away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339629,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined with our measurements for temperature and brightness, we get a radius for Zee of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343710,
    "dDurationMs": 8090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2140 kilometers, plus or minus a few hundred. And that is tiny, even by white dwarf standards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351800,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For comparison, a white dwarf the mass of\nour sun would be around the size of the earth,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356619,
    "dDurationMs": 7491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this new guy is barely 25% bigger than\nthe moon, making it the smallest known white dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364110,
    "dDurationMs": 3529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, one reason that it’s good to know the size of a white dwarf is that it also tells you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367639,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its mass. Here we need to learn something\nthat’s weird about all white dwarfs, not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373310,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just Zee. We normally think about objects\ngetting bigger the more massive they are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377770,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s true of planets and regular stars,\nbut it’s not true of white dwarfs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381819,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In white dwarfs, matter is crushed so close\ntogether that the inward gravitational pull"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387039,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is insane. The only thing holding the star\nup from absolute collapse is the fact that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391969,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it got any smaller, its electrons would\nstart to overlap - they’d have to occupy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396710,
    "dDurationMs": 5859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same energy states. But that’s forbidden by quantum mechanics - specifically, by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402569,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pauli exclusion principle, which tells us\nthat particles in the fermion family, like elelectrons, can never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408189,
    "dDurationMs": 2831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occupy the same quantum state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411020,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In atoms, electrons are held in place by the\ncoulomb force - electrostatic attraction to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416629,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nucleus. And those electrons can occupy discrete energy levels, where the higher the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422149,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, the closer the electron is to escaping the atom. Electrons are bound to the white dwarf"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429509,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by gravity, but they still have discrete energy levels. A forming white dwarf will collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436169,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the electrons are driven down to fill all of the lowest energy states. At that point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441229,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can’t collapse any further because there’s nowhere for the electrons to go."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444699,
    "dDurationMs": 4544,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens if you add more mass to a white dwarf? First let’s think about what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449243,
    "dDurationMs": 5356,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens when you add mass to less weird space-stuff, say a planet or a star. The matter inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454599,
    "dDurationMs": 5365,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is crushed closer together until there’s\nenough pressure to resist the extra gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459964,
    "dDurationMs": 4445,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so the original matter contains a smaller volume. But that doesn’t entirely compensate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464409,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the fact that extra matter is added to\nthe surface. The result is that adding matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468629,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually causes an object to increase in size. But for white dwarfs it’s different - as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474029,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you add mass, the white dwarf has to actually shrink in size in order to have enough pressure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479099,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to resist the extra gravity. As a result,\nthe more massive the white dwarf, the smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484159,
    "dDurationMs": 1150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485309,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if Zee is the smallest known white dwarf it must also be the most massive. Doing a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491179,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little quantum mechanics, it was found that it must weigh in at 1.32 times the Sun’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496659,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass. And that’s a lot, at least for a white dwarf. We’ve known for some time that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501939,
    "dDurationMs": 7211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absolute maximum mass for a white dwarf is 1.44 solar masses - the Chandrasekhar limit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509150,
    "dDurationMs": 5429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Above that and the matter gets packed so close together that one of two things happen. If"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514579,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dying star’s core exceeds the Chandrasekhar\nlimit then it collapses into a neutron star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519820,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or a black hole. But If you\nalready have a white dwarf and then slowly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524060,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add more mass it’ll explode as a type\n1a supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528910,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zee is below this mass limit, so it has avoided destruction - so far. We’ll come back to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535010,
    "dDurationMs": 7019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its final, possibly cataclysmic fate very soon. OK, let’s review the evidence. We have one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542029,
    "dDurationMs": 5461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird white dwarf - it’s extremely massive and compact - but that’s not so strange in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547490,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself. The strange part is that it’s rotating\nextremely quickly and has a crazy strong magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552370,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. We just don’t see these extreme properties in the white dwarfs produced as stars die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558380,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is another mechanism that could\nhave done this - one that we know must happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563600,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe, but that we’ve never seen\nconclusive evidence for it. Zee could be the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569490,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result of a white dwarf collision. If two\nwhite dwarfs are orbiting each other, we expect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575480,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them to slowly spiral together because they emit gravitational radiation that saps away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their orbital energy. We’ve seen the result\nof this with black holes and neutron stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584560,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when LIGO detected the gravitational waves from the last moment of those inspirals. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589459,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it should happen with white dwarfs too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591529,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a pair of white dwarfs merge, two\nthings might happen - either their mass adds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596759,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to more than the Chandrasekhar limit and bad things happen that I’ll come back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601750,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to. Or it adds up to less and we get a bigger, much weirder white dwarf. That star would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607959,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be spinning really really fast because it\ndoesn’t just have the angular momentum from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612459,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its spinning parent stars - it has the angular momentum from the orbits of the parent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618019,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars. This process also explains the intense magnetic fields. Magnetic fields in stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623290,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and planets are generated by dynamos - self-sustaining currents of charged particles. A collision"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629380,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like this could well produce the sort of turbulent motion to jump start a dynamo powerful enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635230,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce the observed magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637060,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have a possible origin story for\nthis white dwarf. Remember I said that we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643060,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should assume typical until proven weird?\nWell that still applies. If this one white"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649250,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dwarf formed this way, that means others probably did also. And it means that white dwarf mergers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655540,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really do happen - which actually has really\nbroad-reaching implications. Remember I said"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there are two things that can happen when a stellar remnant exceeds the Chandrasekhar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666480,
    "dDurationMs": 8390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limit of 1.44 solar masses - absolute collapse for massive stellar cores or absolute explosion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674870,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for accreting white dwarfs. So what happens to merging white dwarfs that exceed this limit?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680850,
    "dDurationMs": 6429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t actually know - it could go either\nway. But if merging white dwarfs DO explode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687279,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it may well be that many of the type\n1a supernovae that we see are NOT from accreting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692940,
    "dDurationMs": 3379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "white dwarfs, as originally believed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696319,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that's true then it may actually affect\nour understanding of the universe on the largest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701980,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales. That’s because observations of type 1a supernovae were how we first discovered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707519,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the existence of dark energy, and we’ve\ntalked about how that was done previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712640,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it turns out that a significant number\nof those supernovae came from merging white"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716930,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dwarfs rather than accreting white dwarfs\nthen perhaps our calculations of the amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721779,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dark energy are wrong. And in case you\nhaven’t been paying attention, there does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727089,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem to be a disagreement between the supernova dark energy measurements and the measurements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732860,
    "dDurationMs": 3789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the cosmic microwave background - again, covered previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736649,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t get me wrong, this issue with the\nsupernovae would not make dark energy go away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741700,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- there’s too much independent evidence\n- but it’s probably something we want to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746470,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort out anyway. OK, let’s get back to Zee. We know how it might have got here, but one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751980,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mystery remains: what is its fate? Will it\njust cool and fade to a black dwarf over trillions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758630,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years? That’s the usual doom of a white\ndwarf. But Zee may get its explosive finale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764540,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after all. A typical white dwarf is pretty\ndense, at around a metric ton per cubic centimeter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770740,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zee is a 1000 times denser still. That means it can support incredibly energetic electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777240,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in its core - electrons so energetic they\nare in danger of slamming into protons, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782940,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would turn those protons into neutrons. If\nthat starts to happen then you get a chain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787339,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaction of so-called electron capture which is how you turn a white dwarf into a neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793289,
    "dDurationMs": 5682,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zee seems to be safe from that happening,\nbut only barely and that may change. Over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798971,
    "dDurationMs": 5178,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millions of years, heavy isotopes - nuclei\nwith more neutrons than protons, will slowly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804149,
    "dDurationMs": 6261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sink, or sediment, to the core. These nuclei\nare more susceptible to electron capture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810410,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So build up enough of that stuff in the center and the electron capture chain reaction may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814670,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "begin - giving us another path to supernova, and a bad end for our highly suspicious little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820920,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821920,
    "dDurationMs": 8469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there we have it- ZTF J1901+1458 - Zee\n- is a moon-sized, highly magnetized white"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830389,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dwarf probably formed when two low mass white dwarfs spiralled into each other. It teeters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835279,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the edge of explosion, and may force us\nto rethink how we measure our universe on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841199,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the largest scales. It’s a glimmer of weirdness that tells us that the universe isn’t quite what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846970,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we thought it was. Perhaps a powerful clue towards a better understanding of this generally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852819,
    "dDurationMs": 1801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854620,
    "dDurationMs": 5259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our Patreon supporters make it possible to\nkeep up the standards of this show. Without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859879,
    "dDurationMs": 10121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your support and enthusiasm, we’d probably have sunk to space-themed\nreaction videos and quantum mechanics based pranks. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870000,
    "dDurationMs": 5249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, thanks for everything. And today’s extra special thank you goes to Charlie, who’s supporting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875249,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us at the big bang level. Charlie, the fabric\nof space around us thrums with the infinitesimal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880889,
    "dDurationMs": 5102,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vibrations of countless white dwarfs that\nhave merged since the beginning of time. With"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885991,
    "dDurationMs": 6528,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the help of your support we’ve deciphered\ntheir song, come to us from a universe away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892519,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that they sing your praises, Charlie, and extend their white dwarf blessings: may your magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898230,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields stay untangled, your electrons\nbe ever degenerate, and may your mass remain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902970,
    "dDurationMs": 2563,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always sub-chandrasekhar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905533,
    "dDurationMs": 7046,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, last time we took a magnetic tour of the universe, exploring how magnetism shapes our cosmos"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912579,
    "dDurationMs": 6981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the scale of planets up to entire galaxies. That magnetic episode .. attracted many questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919560,
    "dDurationMs": 5969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Peter Tiuller asks whether the force that\nstops him when he runs into a wall is ultimately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925529,
    "dDurationMs": 7071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic. Actually it’s not only that. Electromagnetism is responsible for the strong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932600,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bonds between atoms in a solid - so the fact that the wall doesn’t fall apart is due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937199,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to electromagnetism. But the reason you can’t just pass through the wall is something else"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942060,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it’s the Pauli Exclusion principle, which\nsays that electrons in atoms can’t be shoved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947459,
    "dDurationMs": 3961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into each other to occupy the same energy levels. Now we’ll actually be coming back to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951420,
    "dDurationMs": 3811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this when we get into the spin statistic theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955231,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dakronu asks whether magnetic fields have any measurable effect on the orbits of stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959490,
    "dDurationMs": 7709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the galaxy. Not directly. The galactic magnetic field is very weak compared to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967199,
    "dDurationMs": 6691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic fields of stars, or even planets. Stars don’t respond to that field directly. However gas does respond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973890,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the galactic magnetic field - it can definitely move gas around and even trigger gas to collapse into stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979760,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the locations that stars formed may be influenced by magnetic fields, which in turn affects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985420,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their orbits. So the answer is yes,\nsort of."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989100,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexander Charlton and Brandon Munshaw ask whether magnetic field lines are really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994209,
    "dDurationMs": 5641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“lines” versus some sort of continuous\nthing. Well it's definitely the latter - the field lines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999850,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just the way we represent an underlying continuous field. Like Brandon’s example"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004499,
    "dDurationMs": 6491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of isotherms on a weather map or elevation contours. Field lines are continuous, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010990,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the exact choice of where to draw them is\nsomewhat arbitrary. Unlike isotherms and elevation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016870,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contours, they don’t represent lines of\nconstant field. Rather they represent the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020880,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines of steepest gradient in that field.\nThe lines of constant field would be drawn"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025919,
    "dDurationMs": 2841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to intersect the field lines at 90 degrees."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028760,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of you ask questions about the potential role of magnetic fields in the universe that all have the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034049,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "answer I just gave - can magnetic fields be used to explain dark matter or to explain dark energy? Or could magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040819,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields be used to power spacecraft?  So the answer is no, although magnetic fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045160,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do have important effects on scales from stars to galaxies - they’re still much, much weaker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049940,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than gravity. Some people also hopped on to imply that with this episode I’d validated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056330,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electric universe idea - which states\nthat electromagnetism drives the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060850,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the largest scales, and gravity is either\nnegligible or doesn’t exist. … Let me"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be clear - the electric universe pseudo-theory is about as valid as flat earthism - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just dressed up a bit nicer. But I don’t\nwant to get all negative at the end of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video - so instead I”ll direct you to the Professor Dave Explains YouTube channel for a satisfyingly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scathing debunking, along with a brutal analysis of the psychology of pseudoscience conspiracy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088400,
    "dDurationMs": 7890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theories. I will just say this: if electromagnetism really does govern everything, why wasn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096290,
    "dDurationMs": 4999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magneto the most powerful of all mutants? He should have been able to control all gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101289,
    "dDurationMs": 5483,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- not just metal stuff. Personally, that’s enough evidence for me - but if you need more check out Professor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106772,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dave’s episode linked below."
    } ]
  } ]
}
